J['j4'] = {'title': 'Scroll Four · Annals of the Hegemons, Upper',
 'head': 'One chapter, the longest in the book. It runs from the removal of the Zhou capital eastward down to the fall of Sui and the rise of Tang. The main text is barely five thousand characters — a bare chronological skeleton; the author’s own notes run to thirty thousand and put the flesh on it, passage after passage lifted from the histories. The two must be read together: without the notes the main text is hardly a piece of writing at all.'}
C['c17'] = {'title': 'The Hegemon’s Design', 'paras': [
 'The skeleton: Zhou held the empire and governed it for three hundred years and more; under Cheng and Kang punishments were laid aside and not used for forty years. Then the five hegemons rose one after another — a hegemon assists the Son of Heaven, raises what is profitable and removes what is harmful, punishes the violent and forbids the crooked, and sets the sea within to rights in order to honour the Son of Heaven. When the five hegemons were gone and no sage followed them, the Son of Heaven was left weak and alone, his orders were not carried out, the princes did as they pleased, the strong bullied the weak and the many were violent to the few.',
 'Tian Chang usurped Qi and the six great houses divided Jin, and the age of the Warring States began: “this was when the people first began to suffer.” Strong states worked at conquest and weak ones at defence; the vertical and the horizontal alliances formed, chariots ran hub to hub, lice bred in the armour and the people had no one to complain to. Then Qin ate the empire piece by piece and swallowed the warring states, made the government of the sea within one, pulled down the walls of the princes, and its law was severe and its government harsh and flatterers were many. Meng Tian was sent north against the Hu and Wei Tuo south to garrison Yue, troops standing idle in useless country and no one able to live. The First Emperor died and the empire rose in revolt.',
 'And from here the chronicle proper: Chen Sheng and Wu Guang at Chen, Wu Chen and Zhang Er in Zhao, Xiang Liang at Wu, Tian Dan in Qi, Jing Ju at Ying, Zhou Shi in Wei, Han Guang in Yan — the heroes rose out of every gorge and the house of Qin perished. Then Gaozu of Han, a village headman of Pei; the covenant that the first man into Xianyang should be king there; the death of Xiang Liang at Dingtao; Han Xin’s crossing at Jingxing and the battle with the river at his back; Lou Jing urging the capital be put inside the passes; Guangwu at Kunyang; Guandu; the Red Cliff; Liu Yuan and Shi Le; the abdications that made Song, Qi, Liang and Chen; Li Mi and Wang Shichong; and last the Tang.',
 'The notes are where the book lives. Kuai Tong talks the magistrate of Fanyang into surrendering and thirty towns follow “like balls rolling down a slope.” Xiao He tells the king of Han that to be king in Han is at least better than dying, and that the man who can bend below one and stretch above ten thousand is what Tang and Wu were. Li Zuoche offers to cut Han Xin’s supply line and is not listened to, and afterwards Han Xin buys his advice with a thousand pieces of gold. Lou Jing on why the Zhou could hold Luoyang and the Han could not. Zhang Liang on why a capital behind the passes is a hand at the throat and a fist in the back.',
 'The chapter ends with a “discourse” quoting Gan Bao — the rise of a dynasty waits on the mandate of heaven, and if there is succession it is not a human business — and Fan Ye on how great enterprises are lost: the three dynasties by favourite women, Qin by extravagance and cruelty, the Western capital by the consort families, the Eastern by the eunuchs. And then the author’s own summing up, which is not fatalistic at all: <b>from Qin and Han down to Zhou and Sui, those who got the empire got it by getting hold of worthy and outstanding men and raising what profited the people and removing what harmed them; and those who lost it lost it, without exception, by employing small men and by extravagance without measure.</b>',
]}

J['j5'] = {'title': 'Scroll Five · Annals of the Hegemons, Middle',
 'head': 'One chapter, on the Warring States. Nearly the whole of the persuasions of Su Qin and Zhang Yi is copied into the notes, so that the scroll amounts to an abridged Intrigues of the Warring States with a frame around it. The base text of this page had this scroll spliced bodily into the middle of the first note of Scroll Four; it has been cut out and put back where it belongs. See the colophon.'}
C['c18'] = {'title': 'The Seven Powers in Outline', 'paras': [
 'The empire is a great vessel and the living are a heavy store. A vessel that large cannot be managed by one man; a store that heavy cannot be guarded alone. So the wilds were marked off and the borders divided, which is what enfeoffment is for; and near kin and distant were set to hold each other in place, which is what keeps the rise and the fall in check. The Zhou looked at the two dynasties before it, set up five degrees of rank, enfeoffed eight hundred states of which fifty-five were of the royal surname, deepened the root and made the trunk fast so that it could not be pulled up. At the height, the dukes of Zhou and Shao assisted its government; in its decline the five hegemons propped up its weakness. <b>But a law that makes those below thick ends in a tail too big to wag.</b>',
 'After Kings You and Ping it sank year by year; rank and salary came mostly from the retainers of retainers, and campaigns were no longer ordered by the Son of Heaven. Wu was absorbed by Yue, Jin was divided in three, Zheng was swallowed by Han, Lu was destroyed by Chu. For forty years the sea within had no master, and then it was the Warring States. Qin held the ground of advantage and rode a cunning and treacherous army, eating away at the east, and the east was afraid of it. Su Qin of Luoyang bound the princes vertically to keep Qin out; Zhang Yi of Wei broke the vertical league and strung them horizontally. This is where the vertical and the horizontal begin.',
 'Then the persuasions, almost in full. To King Wen of Yan: Yan is safe because Zhao screens it on the south; Qin attacking Yan fights a war a thousand li from home, Zhao attacking Yan fights one a hundred li from home, and a man who worries about the danger at a thousand li and not the one at a hundred has made the worst calculation there is. To Marquis Su of Zhao, to the king of Han — “to serve Qin is to cut off a piece of yourself each year; the land runs out and the demands do not” — to Wei, to Qi, to Chu. And then Zhang Yi, going round the same six courts and undoing each speech in turn. Le Yi’s letter about the flatfish that cannot swim unless it has its pair; Lu Zhonglian refusing to honour Qin as emperor; Fan Ju on befriending the far and attacking the near; Huang Xie’s letter to the king of Qin; Gan Luo, aged twelve, getting five cities out of Zhao by talking.',
 'The chapter ends with a discourse weighing the five degrees of enfeoffment against the commandery system. Zhou lasted eight hundred years and even at the end, reduced to a commoner, the king was still nominally the common lord of all, because the branches held each other up. Qin, having watched Zhou lose its grip, abolished the degrees and set up commanderies; the ruler had the sea within and his own sons were commoners; and the day he died the empire fell apart, because a man in a torn coat could shout in a marsh and there was no prince with any reason to come to the throne’s help. <b>So neither is safe. Where there are commanderies and order, men in plain clothes conceive ambitions; where the five degrees rule an age, there is the danger of the leagues.</b> Every system has its disease. The only conclusion is that the ruler must be anxious, day after day, cultivate his virtue to hold the thing steady and choose worthy men to use — “and then, though there were a sage like Tang or Wu, he could not rise; how much less a man of no account.”',
]}

J['j6'] = {'title': 'Scroll Six · Annals of the Hegemons, Lower',
 'head': 'One chapter, on the Three Kingdoms, and the key is struck in the first paragraph: the reason Wu and Shu could stand against Cao Cao was not virtue or unity but ground. The scroll then works through the geography of the empire region by region and reckons what each is worth for attack and defence. The base text has no general title for this scroll either; “Annals of the Hegemons, Lower” is the Siku editors’ reconstruction from the pattern.'}
C['c19'] = {'title': 'The Leverage of the Three Kingdoms', 'paras': [
 'When the Han lost its grip the net let the fierce through. Yuan Shao looked down from the Yellow River, Liu Biao rose in Jing, Ma Chao and Han Sui held the west of the passes, Lü Bu and Chen Gong seized the east — men with a hundred thousand troops and a thousand squadrons of horse, and every one of them an outstanding man of his day. Yet Cao Cao held the Son of Heaven and gave the princes their orders, and in six or seven years eight or nine out of ten of them had been wiped out. Wu and Shu were little states with the territory of four provinces between them, and their men were timid in a public war and bold in a private brawl, and neither in numbers nor in strength were they a match for the Yuans and Liu Biao and Lü Bu. <b>Why, then, could these two look their enemy in the face and hold him off? Because the ground was not the same, and the leverage made them what they were.</b>',
 'Cao Pi came down to the Yangzi, saw the water running, and said, “This is how heaven keeps north and south apart.” Liu Zi called Nanzheng a prison of heaven and the Xie valley a stone tunnel five hundred li long. Every earlier record is afraid of those depths. The chapter’s own gloss on the famous maxim is dry: heaven’s season is not as good as earth’s advantage, and earth’s advantage is not as good as concord among men — <i>but a middling man holding ground like this could still have drawn out his term</i>.',
 'Then the survey. Shu: five thousand li square, forty commanderies, a metropolis in itself, “the storehouse of heaven,” a thousand li of rich fields, silk enough to clothe the empire, timber, bamboo, fish and salt, iron and copper, and water carriage down to the Yangzi. Li Xiong’s advice to Gongsun Shu; Zhang Song and Fa Zheng bringing Liu Bei in; the road by which Deng Ai came over the mountains at the end. Jing: the throat of the river, which is why Lu Su told Sun Quan to take it and why Lü Meng crossed in the white clothes of a merchant to get it back. Wu: the lakes and the river, and Zhou Yu’s reckoning before the Red Cliff — the northerners are not used to boats, the horses have no pasture, the season is against them, and the illness will do the rest.',
 'The chapter closes with Jia Yi’s Memorial on Security, on why the kings who were near kin rebelled and the king of Changsha, with twenty-five thousand households and no power at all, stayed loyal to the end: “it was not that his nature was different; the shape of things made him so.” And then the author’s own application of it to his own day — <b>the men who hold a walled city now hold a thousand li of it; they are not kin to the throne and they hold ground better than mountains and sea; a man with a state of his own cannot fail to look into this.</b>',
]}

J['j7'] = {'title': 'Scroll Seven · Deliberation on Power',
 'head': 'Two chapters. The first, on taking warning, is the second longest in the book and lays the successions of dynasties out in a line; its conclusion is a single sentence — the empire is not one man’s empire but the empire’s empire. The second shows the same plan producing opposite results in different years.'}
C['c20'] = {'title': 'Taking Warning', 'paras': [
 'The Changes: “Tang and Wu turned the mandate over, in accord with heaven and answering to men.” The Documents: “Soothe me and you are my lord; abuse me and you are my enemy.” The old saying: a cornered rat will bite a wildcat and a common man will rush a lord of ten thousand chariots. The Duke of Yellow Stone: a ruler cannot be without virtue, for without it his ministers rebel.',
 'King Wen at Feng asks the Duke of Tai what to do about the king of Shang, and the answer is the longest single passage in the chapter and the best statement of patience in the book. Cultivate yourself, humble yourself before the worthy, be good to the people, and watch heaven. Until heaven shows a calamity you may not lead; until men show a disaster you may not plan. <b>“The empire is not one man’s empire. Taking the empire is like chasing a wild animal: when it is caught the whole world has a share in the meat.”</b> To take nothing from the people is the way to have the people; to take nothing from the state is the way to have the state. “I look at their fields and the weeds are beating the grain; I look at their crowd and the crooked are beating the straight; I look at their officers and they are savage and rapacious and break the law, and those above do not notice. This is the sign of a state about to fall.”',
 'Then the successions. Shuxiang tells Han Xuanzi why Prince Gan cannot succeed in Chu — there are five difficulties in taking a state: to be favoured and have no men; to have men and no party; to have a party and no plan; to have a plan and no people; to have people and no virtue — and Prince Gan has none of the five, while Qiji has all of them. Zigong goes out once and throws Qi into confusion, preserves Lu, breaks Wu and makes Yue hegemon; Confucius’ comment is uneasy about it. Then Qin’s fall; Wang Mang’s usurpation; Gengshi’s collapse; the end of Sui and the rising at Taiyuan, with the white banner and the oath in the fields.',
 'And the last paragraph, which is why the chapter is called what it is. Confucius, reading the line in the Odes about the officers of Yin pouring the libation in the Zhou capital, sighed and said, “Wealth and honour are not constant. If it were not so, what would princes take warning by and what would the common people be encouraged by?” The Changes: “In safety do not forget danger; in survival do not forget ruin. Thus the person is safe and the state can be kept.” <b>To know fear, and from fear to take warning, is a blessing to a man who has a state.</b>',
]}
C['c21'] = {'title': 'What the Time Calls For', 'paras': [
 'The same thing in two different years is two different things. It is not the things that are perverse; it is the time that has changed.',
 'At the end of Qin, Chen She reached Chen and the local men urged him to take the royal title. Zhang Er and Chen Yu told him not to: “Do not take the title. March west quickly, set up the heirs of the six states, make yourself a party. Then there will be no armies in the field and no walls held against you, and you can punish Qin, hold Xianyang and give the princes their orders. If you make yourself king of Chen alone, the empire will come apart.”',
 'Years later, with Chu and Han deadlocked, Li Yiji gave Gaozu exactly the same advice — set up the heirs of the six states, and their lords and peoples will all wear your virtue. Gaozu said, “Good,” and had the seals cut. Zhang Liang came in and raised <b>eight objections</b>, one after another. Tang enfeoffed the heir of Jie because he could hold Jie’s life in his hand; can you hold Xiang Ji’s? That is the first. King Wu marked Shang Rong’s village, freed Jizi, raised a mound over Bi Gan; can you? That is the second. He opened the granary at Juqiao and scattered the treasure of Lutai; can you? The third. And so to the seventh, which is the one that bites: the wandering gentlemen who left their families and their ancestral graves to follow you are all waiting for a foot of ground. Restore the six states and every one of them goes home to serve his own lord — <b>with whom will you take the empire?</b> Gaozu spat out his food and cursed the scholar and had the seals destroyed.',
 'The chapter then runs the same test on “hold the Son of Heaven and give the princes their orders.” Ju Shou urged it on Yuan Shao, who would not, and Cao Cao did it and won. Du Sichong urged Xiao Yan to fetch the prince of Nankang and set him up, and Zhang Hongce warned that the prince in another man’s hand would make Xiao Yan somebody’s instrument; Xiao Yan refused both, went straight on, took Jiankang and had the south. The same move, and the right answer is different each time. <b>Change with the time; do not hold to one thing.</b>',
]}

